<docs-article
  title="Contributing a Library"
  description="Port any component library to Marko with your AI agent — host it yourself and get listed, or contribute a wanted library to the official repo."
  href="/docs/contributing-a-library"
  toc=TOC
>
  <h2 id="how-it-works">How It Works</h2>

  <p>
    marko-ui grows through <strong>registries</strong>, not monorepo PRs. There
    are two contribution paths:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Community registry (the default)</strong> — port any library,
      host the registry yourself (we provide a template with CI), and get
      listed in the <a href="/docs/directory">Registry Directory</a> with a
      one-line PR. No review queue, no gatekeeping, you own the code.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Official contribution</strong> — a short, curated
      <a href="/docs/directory#wanted-libraries"> wanted list</a> of libraries
      we commit to maintaining in the official repo. Only wanted-list ports
      are accepted as component PRs; everything else belongs in the directory.
      Community registries with real traction can be promoted to the wanted
      list later.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Currently wanted:${" "}
    <for|lib, index| of=directory.wanted by=(lib) => lib.name>
      <a href=lib.homepage target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer external">${lib.name}</a>${index < directory.wanted.length - 1 ? ", " : "."}
    </for>
  </p>

  <h2 id="port-with-an-agent">Port with an AI Agent</h2>

  <p>
    The whole process is packaged as an agent skill that works with any coding
    agent. Install it in your project:
  </p>

  <code-block code=SKILL_INSTALL language="bash"/>

  <p>
    Then prompt your agent: <em>"Port &lt;library&gt; to Marko using the
    port-to-marko skill."</em> The skill routes the port to the right
    destination (it checks the wanted list first), teaches the agent the
    SSR-safe Zag.js pattern and every Marko 6 landmine we hit porting 60+
    components, and — most importantly — makes verification mechanical:
    <code>marko-type-check</code>, SSR + hydration checks on a production
    build, real-pointer-event interaction tests, and screenshot comparison
    against the source library. Agent self-reports are not trusted; evidence
    is.
  </p>

  <h2 id="community-registry">Your Own Registry</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      Clone the${" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/svallory/marko-ui-registry-template" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer external">registry template</a>
      — components.json, an example component, and a GitHub Pages deploy
      workflow are already wired.
    </li>
    <li>Port components into <code>src/ui/</code> (the skill handles this).</li>
    <li>
      <code>bunx marko-ui registry build</code> emits installable${" "}
      <code>public/r/{name}.json</code> payloads;${" "}
      <code>bunx marko-ui registry validate</code> schema-checks them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Push, enable GitHub Pages, and open a PR adding one entry to${" "}
      <code>apps/docs/src/data/directory.json</code>:
    </li>
  </ol>

  <code-block code=DIRECTORY_ENTRY language="json"/>

  <p>
    The compatibility contract is CLI-enforced: <code>target</code> must be${" "}
    <code>"marko"</code> (every listed registry ships Marko source — the CLI
    refuses React-source registries), the <code>url</code> must contain the${" "}
    <code>{name}</code> placeholder, and the registry must be
    open source.
  </p>

  <h2 id="official-contributions">Official Contributions</h2>

  <p>
    For wanted-list libraries only. Fork the repo <strong>before</strong> you
    start porting (the skill does this when it detects a wanted library), work
    on a branch, and follow the repo's own authoring docs
    (<a href="/docs/creating-components">Creating Components</a>,${" "}
    <a href="/docs/zag-adapter">Zag Adapter Anatomy</a>). Components land in${" "}
    <code>packages/shadcn/ui/</code> with per-component${" "}
    <code>registry.meta.json</code>, plus demo pages so the e2e verify-matrix
    covers them.
  </p>

  <p>
    PRs for libraries not on the wanted list will be closed with a pointer to
    the directory path — that's not a rejection of the work, it's where the
    work ships without waiting on us.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parity-gate">The Parity Gate</h2>

  <p>
    Official PRs must pass the repo's mechanical gate before human review:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>bun run check</code> — <code>marko-type-check</code> across every package (never plain <code>tsc</code>, which silently skips <code>.marko</code> files) plus the registry invariant scripts.</li>
    <li><code>bun run test</code> — unit, hydration-invariant, and behavior suites.</li>
    <li><code>bun run build:registry</code> — the registry build must exit 0.</li>
    <li><code>bun run check:parity</code> — the parity-drift toolkit: coverage (every upstream demo and doc section paired by name, or ignored with a reason in <code>parity-ignore.json</code>) and visual diff of paired demos, with <code>interactions.json</code> steps for stateful demos. Exit 3 means drift — read the gallery in <code>parity-report/</code>.</li>
    <li>Production-build browser verification with real pointer events.</li>
    <li>Every deviation logged in the PR description — undisclosed approximations are the one thing that fails review outright.</li>
  </ul>
</docs-article>
